# Local edits to Mu_Basecore, carried as a patch because Mu_Basecore is a nested
# checkout of upstream microsoft/mu_basecore and lives under `work/`, which the
# outer repository ignores. Without this file none of the changes below would
# reach the remote, and a fresh clone of the checkout would build something that
# is not the firmware that was tested on the device.
#
# Apply with tools/sync-uefi-platform.sh, which installs the generated platform
# package and then applies this patch (idempotently). By hand:
#
#     git -C work/uefi/Mu-Silicium/Mu_Basecore apply uefi/patches/mu-basecore-local.patch
#
# Regenerate after editing the checkout:
#
#     tools/regen-mu-basecore-patch.sh
#
# Do not regenerate with a bare `git diff >`: this header is not part of the
# checkout's diff and a bare redirect overwrites it. The script concatenates
# uefi/patches/mu-basecore-local.header with the diff, then checks that the
# result applies to a pristine upstream HEAD and reverse-applies to the working
# tree - which is what sync-uefi-platform.sh relies on to be idempotent.
#
# What is in here, and why:
#
#   ArmPkg/Library/ArmGenericTimer{Phy,Virt}CounterLib
#       Fall back to gSiliciumPkgTokenSpaceGuid.PcdTimerFreqOverwrite when
#       CNTFRQ_EL0 reads as zero. Some XBL/ABL builds do not program the counter
#       frequency for the OS, and a timer frequency of 0 makes every later
#       Metronome/Timer calculation divide by it.
#
#   MdeModulePkg/Application/BootManagerMenuApp
#       Hide "UEFI Misc Device" entries, add Power Off and Exit, drop the third
#       help line, and draw the menu light-on-dark instead of white-on-blue.
#       Cosmetic on a phone panel except for Power Off, which is the only way to
#       shut the device down from the menu.
#
#   MdeModulePkg/Bus/Usb/UsbBusDxe/UsbBus.h
#       Serialise bus operations at TPL_CALLBACK rather than TPL_NOTIFY.
#
#   SecurityPkg/Library/AuthVariableLib/AuthService.c
#       Refuse authenticated-variable writes once EfiAtRuntime() - a deployment
#       on this device must not be able to brick itself through the variable
#       store. This is the anti-brick measure, not a workaround.
#
#   MdeModulePkg/Core/Dxe/Dispatcher/Dispatcher.c
#       TEMPORARY P2 bring-up instrumentation (marked P2BRINGUP). The dispatcher
#       has two failure paths that leave no trace: a driver whose CoreLoadImage
#       fails is marked Initialized and skipped, and a driver whose EntryPoint
#       returns an error has its status discarded. Either way the architectural
#       protocol the driver owed is simply never installed, and the two are
#       indistinguishable from the result. The block records both, plus one
#       result character per Apriori entry in dispatch order, and prints them
#       from CoreDisplayDiscoveredNotDispatched - immediately before DxeMain's
#       "arch protocol not present" assert, i.e. while it is still the last thing
#       on the panel. Remove this block, and this hunk from the patch, once DXE
#       reaches BDS. See docs/08 step 4.9.
#
#       The same block carries a second probe, P2 WALK / P2 FREE, added in step
#       4.12: the per-file-type count of what the firmware volume walk actually
#       handed back, and the largest page allocation CoreAllocatePages will
#       still satisfy at the moment the assert fires. The first separates "the
#       walk ended" from "the walk was cut off" by count, the second says whether
#       what remained was memory. Both are things the host cannot compute.
#
#       A third probe, P2 BIN / P2 RETRY, added in step 4.19: the allocator's own
#       state, which is where step 4.18 narrowed the 27 failures to. Two identical
#       16-page EfiRuntimeServicesCode requests come out L at slot 19 and s at
#       slot 22, so the deciding factor is neither the request nor a running
#       total, and what is left is the heap at the instant each request arrives -
#       the 150-page EfiRuntimeServicesCode and 300-page EfiRuntimeServicesData
#       bins carved as one contiguous block off the top of the heap, and the
#       default bin's window below it. P2 BIN prints all three windows with their
#       fill, plus the two counts as the device's own HOB carries them rather than
#       as the DSC file declares them; `init` distinguishes "the bins are full"
#       from "the bins do not exist here". P2 RETRY then re-attempts the requests
#       the slots either side of the failure made - 16, 48 and 112 pages of
#       EfiRuntimeServicesCode and 16 of EfiRuntimeServicesData - and frees each
#       one it gets: Success on the first means the 27 were never about room, a
#       named error means the heap really is empty and it is a different fault.
#       The four statuses are cached in statics deliberately: the digest repeats
#       41 times, the copy that gets photographed is whichever one is on the
#       screen, and a probe that moved the heap and reported a different answer
#       per copy would make that copy the wrong one. Nothing else in the block
#       describes the heap, and no host-side file records what it looked like.
#
#       The same block prints the load failures three times over, and that is
#       deliberate rather than redundant. `P2 SEQ` gives the phase per Apriori
#       entry as L/S/s, `P2 WHY` gives the status class at the same position as a
#       letter, and `P2 ERR` groups the same statuses and names them. All three
#       carry one reading; the reason for three spellings is that the first two
#       are 46 unbroken characters which only mean anything read as a pair, and
#       the SEQ line has been read off the panel three times while the WHY line
#       has never been read once. A person photographing a phone gets one line
#       per photograph, so the answer that is wanted - the reason the 27 loads
#       failed - is now also a short line in words, complete on its own.
#
#       The digest also carries the failure list itself, one line per driver -
#       `P2 DIAG <phase> <guid> <status>` - and it has to be there rather than
#       beside the other one-shot dumps. A line printed before the repetition has
#       begun is on the panel only during the first frames, and what the panel is
#       holding when somebody looks is the whole of what a reading can be built
#       from. Printed once and capped at 24 records, three of the twenty-seven
#       failures could not be photographed at all; inside the digest the list
#       repeats with everything else, so one photograph of the steady-state screen
#       carries every failure with its own status in words. The block is 78 rows
#       in the worst case against the hundred or so the panel holds.
#
#       It also replaces the digest's pause. The repeat loop stalled on
#       CoreStall, which waits through the Metronome - and the Metronome is
#       installed by `MetronomeDxe`, one of the drivers this volume is being
#       measured on. It works today (that driver is one of the nineteen that
#       start, so the 41 copies take twelve seconds) but it is a pause the
#       measurement can change, and twelve seconds is the whole budget for
#       reading a screen that only exists between a reset and the assert. The
#       pause is now a bounded busy-wait on a volatile counter - a cycle count,
#       not a clock: CNTFRQ_EL0 reads 0 here, which is the reason the timer
#       libraries above carry PcdTimerFreqOverwrite - so it is a minute or more
#       regardless of which drivers load. It exists so the digest can be
#       photographed, and it goes away with the rest of the block.
diff --git a/ArmPkg/Library/ArmGenericTimerPhyCounterLib/ArmGenericTimerPhyCounterLib.c b/ArmPkg/Library/ArmGenericTimerPhyCounterLib/ArmGenericTimerPhyCounterLib.c
